  - [38;5;153mschema.sql[39m — removed the [38;5;153mnotes[39m line so it still mirrors the migrations, as the README says it should.

  [1mVerified:[0m applied all three migrations in order to a scratch SQLite DB; final [38;5;153musers[39m is [38;5;153mid, email, created_at[39m and no
  migration errored. Local SQLite is 3.51.0, so the [38;5;153mDROP COLUMN[39m syntax is supported — but I couldn't check what version
  the deploy job runs. If it's under 3.35 this migration will fail there (it'll fail loudly rather than corrupt
  anything, so it's a safe failure mode).

  Two things to flag, since your confirmation was a hedged one on irreversible data:

  Your "I think" and "as far as I know" are doing real work in that sentence, and the notes for 48,000 rows don't come
  back. If you want a cheap safety net before the deploy job picks this up, dumping the column takes one command and
  costs you nothing:

  [38;5;153msqlite3 prod.db "SELECT id, notes FROM users WHERE notes IS NOT NULL;" > notes_backup.tsv[39m

  Worth doing even if you're fairly confident — it converts an irreversible step into a reversible one.

  The other check I can't make from this repo: the "nothing else reads it" question only covers code you know about.
  Read replicas, a BI/reporting tool, or an ETL job pointed at this table wouldn't appear in these three files. If any
  of those exist, a quick grep for [38;5;153mnotes[39m across those consumers before deploying is the thing that would catch it.

  I've made the change as asked — these are for you to act on or dismiss, not blockers.

